ProsperoSunshine: sunshine.conf turns on getting over a lost frame without a keyframe, and a loss counts once

reference_frame_invalidation (off by default) makes the host announce reference frame invalidation (video_ps5.cpp sets
video::last_encoder_probe_supported_ref_frames_invalidation from it), so a client whose decoder can go on from a frame
after an invalidation names the frames it lost instead of asking for an IDR. For an HEVC stream the host then tells the
console's encoder, which decodes its next frame from the newest picture before the loss, and marks that frame for the
client (video/loss_recovery.hpp; measured on firmware 13.00, docs/re/encoder-options.md section 5). Where that can't
work (H.264, too many frames missing) the client gets an IDR as before.

With it announced, Moonlight names a loss again for every complete frame it has to drop while it waits for the marked
frame: the same first frame, the last one higher each time. The session's counts for the web page and the stream history
took each message as a loss of its own and every frame of every range, so one lost frame that took four frames to answer
counted as four requests and ten frames. Now a loss counts once (a message with another first frame) and each frame once.

